{# `views_filters_summary`, RESTYLED AS `bb_theme:active-filters`.

   WHY A TEMPLATE OVERRIDE AND NO PHP OF OURS. The human's decision of
   2026-09-04: take `views_filters_summary` — which upstream names as the
   replacement for `facets_summary` and the recommended companion of
   `facets_exposed_filters` — rather than keep bakers's own summary code. A
   contrib module's output is restyled by overriding its template, which is what
   this file is: `bb_core` contributes no presenter, no area handler and no
   hook to this screen any more.

   WHAT THE MODULE HANDS OVER, MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED (3.4.0, live
   surface, `?state[]=coding&state[]=review&priority[]=2`). With
   `group_values: true` the `summary` array carries ONE ENTRY PER FACET, keyed
   `id` (the exposed identifier, `state`), `label` (the exposed label, `State`)
   and `groups` — one entry per chosen value, each with the DISPLAY value the
   facet's own build processors resolved (`Coding`, not `coding`) and a `link`
   render array. Without `group_values` it is one entry per VALUE, which cannot
   feed a component whose chip is one facet with several values, so the option
   is load-bearing rather than cosmetic.

   THE VALUES OF ONE FACET THEREFORE ARRIVE TOGETHER, which is what lets the
   component derive its own operator: `active-filters.twig` reads `is` from one
   value and `is either of` from several. Measured on the rendered page:
   `State is either of Coding, Review` and `Priority is Medium`.

   THE PER-FIELD OPERATOR OVERRIDE IS BACK, BY A DIFFERENT ROUTE, and the route
   it used to take really is dead. `Labels` read `includes any of` from a
   `summary_operator` on its BEF widget. The `views_filters_summary` theme hook
   declares exactly three variables — `summary`, `options`, `exposed_form_id` —
   and `options` holds the AREA's own settings; nothing in either names a
   filter's widget configuration, and a summary entry carries only `id`, `label`
   and `groups` (`buildFilterSummaryGroupedItem()` builds a fresh array from
   those three, so even a key added by
   `hook_views_filters_summary_info_alter()` is discarded). There is no path
   from a widget's settings to this template, so THAT override would need a hook
   of ours — which the same decision refused, and `summary_operator` stays
   deleted rather than left in a settings form nothing reads.

   WHAT THERE IS A PATH FOR IS THE FACET'S OWN IDENTIFIER. `id` is the exposed
   identifier the checkboxes submit, it is one of the three keys a summary entry
   does carry, and `active-filters` has always accepted a per-facet `operator`
   prop — the prop predates the module. So the word is stated HERE, by the
   caller, keyed on the identifier: `bb_operators` below. That is the same
   kind of decision as `empty_label` two lines further down — a rendering word
   this template owns because it is the one calling the component — rather than
   a value the view carries. A facet the map does not name gets NO `operator`
   key at all, so the component keeps deriving `is` / `is either of` from the
   number of values, which is where that logic belongs.

   REMOVAL IS AN ADDRESS, NOT A SCRIPT, AND THAT IS THIS FILE'S ONE PIECE OF
   REAL WORK. The module's own dismiss link is `Url::fromUserInput('/')` plus
   `data-remove-selector="<identifier>:<raw value>"`, and its JavaScript
   unchecks the matching exposed control and calls `location.reload()` — a full
   page load, per VALUE, with `form_build_id` and `form_id` swept into the
   address by `new FormData(form)`. `bb_theme:active-filters` renders a chip's
   `×` as an ANCHOR over a `remove_url`, the region this renders inside is
   boosted so following an anchor swaps that region rather than reloading, and
   the chip's sentence is about the whole facet. So the addresses are built here
   from the set the module just stated: the current path plus every OTHER
   facet's values, in the `id[]=value` spelling the exposed checkboxes submit,
   so a shared link reads the same however it was reached.

   THE RAW VALUES COME OUT OF `data-remove-selector`, and that is ugly on
   purpose rather than by accident. A summary entry states the DISPLAY value
   (`Coding`) and the raw one (`coding`) only inside that attribute, which is
   the module's own removal contract — its JavaScript reads exactly this
   attribute to learn which control to uncheck. An address has to carry the raw
   value, so the attribute is the only source there is; the alternative was a
   PHP hook, which is what this whole file exists to avoid.

   THE UNFILTERED CASE NEVER REACHES THIS FILE, and that is still true — what
   changed on 2026-09-04 is who states it. `buildFilterSummary()` returns `[]`
   when no filter has a value, `buildFilterSummaryMarkup()` then returns NULL,
   and the area renders an empty string, so this theme hook is never invoked at
   all. No hook of ours and no Views option changes that.

   SO THE STATEMENT MOVED OUT OF THIS FILE AND UP ONE LEVEL. This file now
   renders the `chips_only` VARIANT of `active-filters` — the chip sentences and
   nothing else, no wrapper — and the view's own `display_builder` tree wraps
   the whole `view_header` in an OUTER `active-filters` node that fills its
   `chips` slot. That outer node owns the wrapper, the accessible name and
   `empty_label`, and it states `empty_label` exactly when the slot renders no
   text — which is precisely the case the module cannot report. `label` and
   `empty_label` are therefore NOT passed from here any more; passing them would
   be a second caller claiming the same words, and the outer one can say
   different words per display (`All tickets` on the full list, `My open
   tickets` on `my_work`) where this file can only say one.

   WHAT THAT ARRANGEMENT COSTS, stated because it is a real loss. The outer node
   cannot tell "no filter has a value" from "every value present is one the
   facet cannot name", because both reach it as an empty slot. So
   `?label[]=999999` reads `All tickets` above a list narrowed to nothing.
   `TicketFacetLabelsTest::testAnIdNothingResolvesStatesTheUnfilteredWordAndNoChip`
   asserts that, with the reasoning for accepting it.

   ONE MORE THING THE SEAM COSTS, AND THE COMPONENT NOW CARRIES THE REPAIR.
   `Result::render()` returns `['#markup' => str_replace(…)]` — a plain PHP
   string, so `Renderer::ensureMarkupIsSafe()` puts everything below through
   `Xss::filterAdmin()`. That list has no `svg` and no `use`, so the sprite icon
   `active-filters` used to draw inside the `×` anchor was STRIPPED and the
   anchor rendered empty; and attribute values are protocol-filtered, so
   `aria-label="Remove filter: State"` arrived as `" State"`. Both were measured
   on the live page and both are FIXED — in the component, which is where they
   are emitted: the glyph is a text `×` no XSS filter has a reason to remove,
   and the accessible name reads `Remove the State filter`, which has no colon
   for `filterBadProtocol()` to mistake for a scheme. The `::after` rule in
   `css/app.src.css` that used to put the glyph back is deleted with them.
#}
{% set bb_path = path('<current>') %}

{# One entry per facet: what it is called, what reads, and the RAW values an
   address has to carry. `item.groups` is the grouped shape; the `[item]`
   fallback keeps this template honest if the option is ever turned off. #}
{% set bb_applied = [] %}
{% for item in summary %}
  {% set bb_values = [] %}
  {% set bb_raws = [] %}
  {% for entry in item.groups|default([item]) %}
    {% set bb_values = bb_values|merge([entry.value ~ '']) %}
    {% set bb_selector = attribute(entry.link, '#attributes')['data-remove-selector']|default('') ~ '' %}
    {% if bb_selector is not empty %}
      {% set bb_raws = bb_raws|merge([bb_selector|split(':', 2)|last]) %}
    {% endif %}
  {% endfor %}
  {% set bb_applied = bb_applied|merge([{
    'id': item.id ~ '',
    'field': item.label ~ '',
    'values': bb_values,
    'raws': bb_raws,
  }]) %}
{% endfor %}

{# THE FACETS WHOSE OWN VOCABULARY DIFFERS FROM THE DERIVED ONE, keyed by the
   exposed identifier. Only `label` is in here, and that is the whole point of a
   map rather than a flag: `Labels` is the one facet on this screen whose values
   are a SET the ticket may hold several of, so `includes any of` says something
   `is either of` does not — the ticket carries any of these, not is one of
   them. Every other facet is a single-valued field where the derived operator
   is right, and a facet absent from this map gets no `operator` key at all. #}
{% set bb_operators = {
  'label': 'includes any of'|t,
} %}

{# The chip set: each facet's sentence plus the address of this list WITHOUT it.
   Built from the applied set rather than from the request, so a link cannot
   carry a filter the chips do not show. #}
{% set bb_facets = [] %}
{% for facet in bb_applied %}
  {% set bb_query = [] %}
  {% for other in bb_applied %}
    {% if other.id != facet.id %}
      {% for raw in other.raws %}
        {% set bb_query = bb_query|merge([other.id|url_encode ~ '[]=' ~ raw|url_encode]) %}
      {% endfor %}
    {% endif %}
  {% endfor %}
  {% set bb_chip = {
    'field': facet.field,
    'values': facet.values,
  } %}
  {# `|default(true)` treats boolean false as empty, so a View that turns
     the remove control off would still emit `id[]=value` addresses. #}
  {% if options.show_remove_link is not defined or options.show_remove_link %}
    {% set bb_chip = bb_chip|merge({
      'remove_url': bb_query is empty ? bb_path : bb_path ~ '?' ~ bb_query|join('&'),
    }) %}
  {% endif %}
  {# MERGED RATHER THAN SET TO NULL. `operator` is typed `string`, and SDC's
     prop validation runs whenever `twig.config.debug` is on, so an explicit
     null would be a validation failure on exactly the surfaces that check. #}
  {% if bb_operators[facet.id] is defined %}
    {% set bb_chip = bb_chip|merge({'operator': bb_operators[facet.id]}) %}
  {% endif %}
  {% set bb_facets = bb_facets|merge([bb_chip]) %}
{% endfor %}

{{ include('bb_theme:active-filters', {
  variant: 'chips_only',
  facets: bb_facets,
}, with_context = false) }}
